    #      carried) and of the carried case, each on its own check line, the
    #      plant turning its own flag; every flag has a plant on both cases.
    #      The xml values check wants some value written (the workbook's new
    #      sheet; change LF's form).
    #  XJ. The run loop, the record pair, the keep and the open check are VJ's
    #      with this phase's names; the record's families are derived from its
    #      entries table, which the rule checked row by row; four record files
    #      installed, two when the keep is skipped.
    #  XK. Ruling 8 of the planning rulings during P2 (build pruning) and
    #      ruling 10 of the planning rulings during P3 (one target root per
    #      phase, one subdirectory per source tree): the phase's target root is
    #      <target>, <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p6-p3/target, and every build
    #      of <repo> by this script, dry or logged, goes into <target>/main
    #      (CARGO_TARGET_DIR), the subdirectory ruling 10 gives the main tree;
    #      this script builds from no other tree. P2's <build> placeholder (a
    #      run directory holding its own target) is gone with the input
    #      RUN_BUILD_ROOT, so no run moves the builds, and nothing here cleans
    #      them; the i686 keep is <work>/keep-i686, kept, not under the target.
    #      VK stands: this script runs no `rm -rf`.
    #
    #
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 6 P4 from M3 part 6 P3's run script (at the phase
    # commit 6a90674, printed in records/logs/m3p6-p3/run.log; its change lists
    # up to XK are above), generated by <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p6-p4/gen/
    # run.py, every replacement asserted to occur as often as it says, the top
    # summary replaced, the build section replaced by the size log's binaries,
    # the copy sample's record pair carried into the earlier samples' loop by
    # its anchor lines, and the body grepped afterwards for P3's names,
    # subjects and tokens. copycmp.py and copysurvey.py are P3's, copied beside
    # this script byte for byte. What differs, and why:
    #  YA. The phase, the log directory, the subjects, the script directory
    #      (<scripts>, <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p6-p4/scripts, on the disk)
    #      and the keep (m3p6-p4/keep-host) are this phase's; the base is P3's
    #      record commit ("M3 part 6 P3 record:"); the phase commit is P4's
    #      catalogue and probes commit by its subject ("M3 part 6 P4: the
    #      catalogue and limits, the probes"); the four samples each have a run
    #      directory <runs>/<date>-<sha>-<sample> and a record stem
    #      m3p6-p4-<sample>-<date>-<sha> (the spec's names, "P4"); SAMPLES is
    #      still the copy sample, whose checks are P3's, and RERUNS the three
    #      earlier samples; SUPERSEDED_RUN names the four by their common stem;
    #      the spec pinned through SPEC_SHA at P4's doc commit, planning's
    #      rulings during P4 standing uncommitted in the working tree's copy,
    #      printed. The usage takes the size.log as its third argument.
    #  YB. Change XB's form: the committed open-check record the keep is
    #      compared with is P3's, the last committed, named by its record
    #      commit, resolved by its subject ("M3 part 6 P3 record:"; no hash is
    #      typed here); it is the base itself, the parent of P4's doc commit; the
    #      control names the doc commit instead. P3_REC names the same commit,
    #      the one whose run directories the four samples are compared with
    #      (change YF).
    #  YC. Part 5 P3's change RC: the release binaries are the size log's, run
    #      where it built them (<target>/main/release and
    #      <target>/main/i686-unknown-linux-musl/release, the main tree's subdirectory, ruling 10
    #      of the planning rulings during P3), each hashed here and compared with
    #      its "hash: <name> <target> <sha256>" line in the size.log given, none
    #      built here; the size.log checked to be this phase's at this phase
    #      commit and to end all as expected. The run row's binaries are checked
    #      against the same hashes as before.
    #  YD. The survey (change XD) and the comparison (change XC) are P3's, byte
    #      for byte, run over the copy sample as P3's run did.
    #  YE. The earlier samples' rows: every row ok, every input and output
    #      opening in LibreOffice with no leftover, and the summary's status
    #      totals the entries table's; their entry rules are P2's and P1's, and
    #      hold here because every diff table is P3's run's byte for byte
    #      (change YF), whose run log checked them against P2's runs, which
    #      checked the rules.
    #  YF. Change XF's form for four samples: every sample, the copy sample
    #      included, compared on both targets with P3's run directory for it,
    #      its outputs by sha256, its lines and its diff tables byte for byte,
    #      every workbook's outcome and exit equal; the run directories read
    #      from the "the run directories:" line of P3's run log at P3's record
    #      commit and checked against the run directory P3's committed copy
    #      record names.
    #  YJ. Change XJ's record pair for each of the four samples: the copy
    #      sample's as P3's, and the three earlier samples' through the same
    #      record script and record check, their families derived from their
    #      entries tables; ten record files installed, eight when the keep is
    #      skipped.
    #  YK. Ruling 8 of the planning rulings during P2 and ruling 10 of those
    #      during P3: the phase's target root is <target>, <home>/.cache/
    #      emend-build/m3p6-p4/target; the keep's tests build into <target>/main
    #      (CARGO_TARGET_DIR), where size.log built the release binaries this
    #      log runs; nothing here cleans it. VK stands: this script runs no
    #      `rm -rf`.
    #
    #
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 7 P3 from M3 part 6 P4's run script (at the phase
    # commit 9cfd92e, printed in records/logs/m3p6-p4/run.log; its change lists
    # up to YK are above), generated by <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-p3/gen/
    # run.py, every replacement asserted to occur as often as it says, the top
    # summary replaced, the keep and open-check sections removed, and the body
    # grepped afterwards for part 6 P4's names, subjects and tokens.
    # copycmp.py and copysurvey.py are part 6 P4's, copied beside this script
    # byte for byte. What differs, and why:
    #  RNA. The phase, the log directory, the subjects and the script directory
    #       (<scripts>, <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-p3/scripts, on the disk)
    #       are this phase's; the base is part 7 P2's record commit ("M3 part 7
    #       P2 record:"); the phase commit is P3's code commit by its subject
    #       ("M3 part 7 P3: rust-version equal to the pin, and the gate checks
    #       the pins agree"); the four samples each have a run directory
    #       <runs>/<date>-<sha>-<sample> and a record stem
    #       m3p7-p3-<sample>-<date>-<sha> (specs/0029 "Harness runs"); the spec
    #       is specs/0029, pinned through SPEC_SHA at this phase's doc commit. The usage
    #       takes the size.log as its second argument, and no test.log.
    #  RNB. The run directories the four samples are compared with are part 6
    #       P4's, named by its record commit, resolved by its subject ("M3 part 6
    #       P4 record:"; no hash is typed here), an ancestor of the phase commit,
    #       read from the "the run directories:" line of part 6 P4's run log at
    #       that commit and checked against the run directory its committed copy
    #       record names.
    #  RNC. The keep and the open check go, with their inputs (RUN_KEEP,
    #       RUN_SKIP_KEEP, SUPERSEDED_KEEP), the test.log argument, the <keep>
    #       root and the open record's stem: specs/0029 "Harness runs" names the
    #       four samples alone. The record files installed are the four pairs,
    #       eight.
    #  RND. Each sample's record header names its binaries (specs/0029 item 5):
    #       the .md holds one "- Binaries (host)" and one "- Binaries (i686)"
    #       line whose two hashes are the binaries this log ran, with a control.
    #  RNE. The size.log is this phase's (its "about:" line names P3's phase
    #       commit).
    #  RNF. The phase's target root is <target>, <home>/.cache/emend-build/
    #       m3p7-p3/target (specs/0001 "Rules for every phase", one target root
    #       per phase); this log builds nothing and runs the release binaries
    #       size.log built in <target>/main.
    #  RNG. The runner gives each child its own TMPDIR under its own work
    #       directory, under <work>/tmp-run here (specs/0029 P1 ruling 2); the
    #       check that <work>/tmp-run is empty after each run stands.
    #
    #
    # Usage: m4p2-p1-run.sh <phase commit> > <file outside the tree>; the caller
    # moves the output to records/logs/m4p2-p1/run.log.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$SELF_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$SELF_DIR/out/run.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    unset EMEND_CORPUS_DIR EMEND_KEEP_DIR
    export RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never
    BUILD_ROOT=${RUN_BUILD:-$HOME/.cache/emend-build/m4p2-p1/run}
    SUPERSEDED_DIR=$HOME/.cache/emend-build/m4p2-p1/superseded
    CHECKER=$SELF_DIR/readme_check.py
    CLCHECK=$SELF_DIR/changelog_check.py
    P1CHECKS=$SELF_DIR/p1_checks.sh
    SPEC=specs/0031-release-metadata.md
    SPEC_WANT=18cab49d4c6906a7a2b37ecddcc88da03b372410832cdf0484f7e1247bb4c44a
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT" || exit 2
    B=$(mktemp -d -p "$BUILD_ROOT") || exit 2
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$B/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$B/counting.sh"
    fail=0
    check() { echo "  $1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    # For a condition that must NOT hold: a leading bang inside yn runs a
    # command named by the bang (specs/0001), so a negation has its own helper.
    ynnot() { if "$@"; then echo no; else echo yes; fi; }
    
    head_is() { [ "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$1" ]; }
    full_sha() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'; }
    # The given commit is the phase commit when it is a full sha and HEAD.
    given_ok() { full_sha "$1" && head_is "$1"; }
    if given_ok "$C"; then :; else echo "the phase commit must be a full 40-hex sha that is HEAD, not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    case "${C: -1}" in 0) LAST=1 ;; *) LAST=0 ;; esac
    FAKE_SHA=${C:0:39}$LAST
    SHORT_SHA=${C:0:7}
    # This phase's logs and planning's uncommitted spec (change GA).
    OURS='^((\?\?| ?[MARCD]) records/logs/m4p2-p1/| M specs/0031-release-metadata[.]md$)'
    entry_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    entry_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    entry_clean() { [ "$entry_all" != error ] && [ "$entry_ours" != error ] && [ "$entry_all" = "$entry_ours" ]; }
    if entry_clean; then :; else
      echo "the working tree holds changes outside this phase's logs and planning's uncommitted spec" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    
    scrub() {
      sed -e "s#$MAIN#<repo>#g" -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$B#<scratch>/w#g" \
        -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<build>#g" -e "s#$SUPERSEDED_DIR#<superseded>#g" \
        -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    # The environment and path inputs, each on its labelled line.
    echo "input: RERUN_NOTE: ${RERUN_NOTE:-unset}"
    echo "input: RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset) (its value never printed)"
    echo "input: HOME: set, read for the paths under it, printed as <home>"
    echo "input: RUN_BUILD: ${RUN_BUILD:-unset} (the build root, <build>)"
    echo "about: M4 part 2 P1 phase commit $C; HEAD at run: $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo
    echo "## the three shape greps, over this script, before its log (specs/0001)"
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|ynnot|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$B/shape-fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$B/shape-fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      check "$shape: this script holds none" "$(yn [ "$n_self" = 0 ])"
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$n_fix" = 1 ])"
    done
    check "control: ynnot reports no on a passing command" "$(yn [ "$(ynnot true)" = no ])"
    
    echo
    echo "## the phase's commits by role, read from git"
    by_subject() { git log --format='%H' -n 1 -F --grep="$1"; }
    REC=$(by_subject 'M4 part 1 close-out record: the push, the CI run and the scan')
    DOC1=$(by_subject 'M4 part 2 P1: commit planning docs (the part 1 close-out verification and rulings, specs/0031)')
    CODE1=$(by_subject 'M4 part 2 P1: CHANGELOG.md, the unreleased section written from the specs and records')
    CODE2=$(by_subject 'M4 part 2 P1: the crate-level doc comment rewritten from the README')
    CODE3=$(by_subject 'M4 part 2 P1: the README and the licence texts in the package')
    echo "command: git log --format=%H -n 1 -F --grep=<the role's subject>, for each role"
    echo "  M4 part 1's close-out record commit (the base): ${REC:-none}"
    echo "  the doc commit: ${DOC1:-none}"
    echo "  the first code commit (CHANGELOG.md): ${CODE1:-none}"
    echo "  the second code commit (the doc comment): ${CODE2:-none}"
    echo "  the third code commit (the README and the licence texts in the package, the phase commit): ${CODE3:-none}"
    echo "command: git rev-parse <phase commit>~0 to <phase commit>~4"
    for k in 0 1 2 3 4; do git rev-parse "$C~$k"; done | sed 's/^/    /'
    roles_in_order() {
      local r k=0
      for r in "$CODE3" "$CODE2" "$CODE1" "$DOC1" "$REC"; do
        [ -n "$r" ] || return 1
        [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~$k")" = "$r" ] || return 1
        k=$((k + 1))
      done
    }
    check "the three code commits, the doc commit and the base, each the next one's parent, the third code commit the phase commit (change GA)" "$(yn roles_in_order)"
    roles_ctl() { local CODE3=$DOC1; roles_in_order; }
    check "control: roles_in_order refuses the doc commit named as the phase commit" "$(ynnot roles_ctl)"
    echo
    echo "phase: Milestone 4 part 2 P1 ($SPEC) run.log; run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    if [ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ]; then
      echo "rerun: $RERUN_NOTE"
      echo "rerun: the superseded files are kept in <superseded>"
      echo "command: sha256sum and wc -c over each superseded attempt named above"
      for sf in ${RERUN_FILES:-}; do
        if [ -f "$sf" ]; then
          printf '    %s  %s bytes  %s\n' "$(sha256sum "$sf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(wc -c <"$sf")" "$(basename "$sf")"
        else
          printf '    NO HASH EXISTS for %s: the file is not there at run time\n' "$(basename "$sf")"
        fi
      done
      if [ -z "${RERUN_FILES:-}" ]; then echo "    RERUN_FILES was not set, so no attempt above is hashed by this run"; fi
    fi
    n_cargo=$(pgrep -x cargo | wc -l)
    n_rustc=$(pgrep -x rustc | wc -l)
    echo "SERIALIZED: no other log run was in flight; cargo processes at entry: $n_cargo; rustc processes at entry: $n_rustc; this log builds nothing, runs no binary and reads no corpus: it reads files of the phase commit and the base by git show (change GB)"
    no_build_running() { [ "$((n_cargo + n_rustc))" = 0 ]; }
    check "no cargo or rustc process was running at entry" "$(yn no_build_running)"
    echo "root: <repo> stands for the repository's top level (inside <home>, so scrubbed first); scripts/counting.sh, README.md, CHANGELOG.md, crates/emend/src/lib.rs, crates/emend/Cargo.toml, the four licence texts, records/logs/m3p7-p4/run.log and $SPEC are read from it by git show at the phase commit (lib.rs also at the base), the doc comment's commit is diffed in it, and the working spec is hashed in it; nothing in it is run or built"
    echo "root: <home> stands for the home directory of the account that ran this script; nothing under it is read but the roots below"
    echo "root: <scripts> stands for <home>/.cache/emend-build/m4p2-p1/scripts, this script's own directory on the disk, which holds the script, the README check program readme_check.py, the CHANGELOG check program changelog_check.py, the shared checks p1_checks.sh (sourced) and, in <scripts>/out, its own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    echo "root: <build> stands for the build root on the disk, <home>/.cache/emend-build/m4p2-p1/run unless RUN_BUILD names another; nothing is built there"
    echo "root: <superseded> stands for <home>/.cache/emend-build/m4p2-p1/superseded, where a rerun's superseded files are kept; read only"
    echo "root: <scratch>/w stands for this run's mktemp -d working directory under <build>, kept on the disk after the run: <scratch>/w/counting.sh the phase commit's library, sourced; the files read by git show, the extracts and the controls' copies beside it"
    echo "root: <corpus> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change GB)"
    echo "root: <runs> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change GB)"
    echo "root: <target> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change GB)"
    echo "root: <work> stands for a directory of an earlier phase's log and appears in this log only in the printed script's carried history; this log reads and writes nothing under it (change GB)"
    apart() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 1 ;; esac; case "$2/" in "$1"/*) return 1 ;; esac; return 0; }
    roots_distinct() { apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$MAIN" && apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$MAIN" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT" && apart "$SUPERSEDED_DIR" "$MAIN"; }
    check "the roots other than <home> are distinct, none inside another (<scratch>/w inside <build>)" "$(yn roots_distinct)"
    roots_ctl() { apart "$B" "$BUILD_ROOT"; }
    check "control: apart refuses <scratch>/w against <build>, which holds it" "$(ynnot roots_ctl)"
    echo "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY set in this shell: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    key_unset() { [ -z "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ]; }
    check "ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is not set" "$(yn key_unset)"
    key_set_ctl() { local ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=planted; key_unset; }
    check "control: the same check refuses a shell where the variable is set" "$(ynnot key_set_ctl)"
    echo "at entry: status lines $entry_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's two files $entry_ours; the lines:"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    echo "  the phase commit given to this script: $C"
    echo "  git rev-parse HEAD at this run:        $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    echo "  the controls' commits: the null commit $NULL_SHA; the fabricated commit $FAKE_SHA (the given one, its last hex digit changed); the short sha $SHORT_SHA"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is a full sha" "$(yn full_sha "$C")"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is HEAD" "$(yn head_is "$C")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the null commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$NULL_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a fabricated commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$FAKE_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses a short sha of the real commit" "$(ynnot given_ok "$SHORT_SHA")"
    short_names_head() { [ "$(git rev-parse "$SHORT_SHA")" = "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" ]; }
    check "control: the short sha is the real commit's, so only its form is refused" "$(yn short_names_head)"
    ours_ctl() { printf ' M scripts/check.sh\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified gate file" "$(ynnot ours_ctl)"
    ours_readme_ctl() { printf ' M CHANGELOG.md\n' | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "$OURS"; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern refuses a modified CHANGELOG.md" "$(ynnot ours_readme_ctl)"
    ours_rec_ctl() { printf '?? records/logs/m4p2-p1/run.log\n?? records/logs/m4p1-p3/run.log\n?? records/m4p2-p1-x-2026-01-01-abcdef0.md\n?? records/logs/m4p2-p1x/run.log\n M ROADMAP.md\n M specs/0031-release-metadata.md\n' | count_matches -E "$OURS" /dev/stdin; }
    check "control: the phase's status pattern takes this phase's log and planning's spec alone, no other phase's log, no record, no sibling directory and no ROADMAP.md (change GA)" "$(yn [ "$(ours_rec_ctl)" = 2 ])"
    SPEC_HAVE=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_BLOB=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum: $SPEC_BLOB; sha256sum $SPEC (the working file, printed and not asserted: planning holds its rulings during P1 uncommitted; change GA): $SPEC_HAVE"
    check "the spec's blob at the phase commit is at the hash this log pins" "$(yn [ "$SPEC_BLOB" = "$SPEC_WANT" ])"
    spec_ctl() { [ "$(printf 'x' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$SPEC_WANT" ]; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the hash of another file" "$(ynnot spec_ctl)"
    echo
    echo "## script that wrote this log"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$SELF"
    echo
    echo "## the shared counting library this log sources"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/counting.sh"
    echo
    echo "## the check programs and the shared checks (changes GC and GD)"
    echo "command: sha256sum <scripts>/readme_check.py: $(sha256sum <"$CHECKER" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    RC_HAVE=$(sha256sum <"$CHECKER" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    git show "$C:records/logs/m3p7-p4/run.log" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E '^command: sha256sum <scripts>/readme_check[.]py: [0-9a-f]{64}$' | sed 's/.*: //' >"$B/rc-want"
    n_rc_want=$(count_lines "$B/rc-want")
    RC_WANT=$(head -n 1 "$B/rc-want")
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:records/logs/m3p7-p4/run.log | grep -o -E '^command: sha256sum <scripts>/readme_check[.]py: [0-9a-f]{64}\$' -> lines $n_rc_want; the hash: $RC_WANT"
    check "M3 part 7 P4's run log prints the README check program's hash once" "$(yn [ "$n_rc_want" = 1 ])"
    check "the README check program here is that program, byte for byte by its sha256 (change GC)" "$(yn [ "$RC_HAVE" = "$RC_WANT" ])"
    rc_hash_ctl() { [ "$(sha256sum <"$CLCHECK" | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$RC_WANT" ]; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the CHANGELOG check program's hash" "$(ynnot rc_hash_ctl)"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$CHECKER"
    echo "command: sha256sum <scripts>/changelog_check.py: $(sha256sum <"$CLCHECK" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$CLCHECK"
    echo "command: sha256sum <scripts>/p1_checks.sh (sourced; the negative log sources the same file): $(sha256sum <"$P1CHECKS" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$P1CHECKS"
    . "$P1CHECKS"
    p1_fns() { declare -F p1_bullets >/dev/null && declare -F p1_readme_measured >/dev/null && declare -F p1_measured_equal >/dev/null && declare -F p1_width_ok >/dev/null && declare -F p1_doc_lines_only >/dev/null && declare -F p1_docblock >/dev/null && declare -F p1_paragraphs >/dev/null; }
    check "the shared checks' seven functions are defined after sourcing" "$(yn p1_fns)"
    p1_fns_ctl() { declare -F p1_no_such_function >/dev/null; }
    check "control: the same test refuses a function the file does not define" "$(ynnot p1_fns_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## counting controls"
    counting_controls
    if [ "$counting_controls_ok" != yes ]; then fail=1; fi
    
    echo
    echo "## the files read at the phase commit and at the base (change GB)"
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:<path> > <scratch>/w/<name>, for each path below; git show <base>:crates/emend/src/lib.rs > <scratch>/w/lib-base.rs; git ls-tree -r --name-only <phase commit> > <scratch>/w/tree.txt"
    n_read=0
    read_one() {
      git show "$1:$2" >"$B/$3" && [ -s "$B/$3" ] && n_read=$((n_read + 1))
      echo "  $2 at $4 -> <scratch>/w/$3: $(wc -c <"$B/$3") bytes, $(wc -l <"$B/$3") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/$3" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    }
    read_one "$C" README.md README.md "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" CHANGELOG.md CHANGELOG.md "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" crates/emend/src/lib.rs lib.rs "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" crates/emend/Cargo.toml Cargo.toml "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" LICENSE-MIT LICENSE-MIT "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" LICENSE-APACHE LICENSE-APACHE "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" crates/emend/LICENSE-MIT crate-LICENSE-MIT "the phase commit"
    read_one "$C" crates/emend/LICENSE-APACHE crate-LICENSE-APACHE "the phase commit"
    read_one "$REC" crates/emend/src/lib.rs lib-base.rs "the base"
    git ls-tree -r --name-only "$C" >"$B/tree.txt"
    echo "  the tree listing at the phase commit: $(count_lines "$B/tree.txt") paths"
    check "the nine files were read and none is empty" "$(yn [ "$n_read" = 9 ])"
    R=$B/README.md
    CL=$B/CHANGELOG.md
    
    echo
    echo "## 1. the citation check over README.md and CHANGELOG.md (specs/0031 \"The CHANGELOG (P1)\"; changes GC and GE)"
    echo "command: python3 -I <scripts>/readme_check.py <scratch>/w/README.md <scratch>/w/tree.txt"
    python3 -I "$CHECKER" "$R" "$B/tree.txt" >"$B/rc.out" 2>"$B/rc.err" </dev/null
    rc=$?
    echo "  exit: $rc; standard error $(wc -c <"$B/rc.err") bytes; standard output:"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/rc.out"
    summary_is() { tail -n 1 "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE "^sentences [0-9]+ with-digit [0-9]+ cited [0-9]+ $2\$"; }
    rc_ok() { [ "$rc" = 0 ] && summary_is "$B/rc.out" 'uncited 0 missing 0' && [ ! -s "$B/rc.err" ]; }
    echo "  README.md: $(tail -n 1 "$B/rc.out")"
    check "README.md: every sentence holding a digit cites a records/ path the phase commit's tree holds: exit 0, uncited 0 missing 0, standard error empty" "$(yn rc_ok)"
    with_digit=$(tail -n 1 "$B/rc.out" | awk '{print $4}')
    cited=$(tail -n 1 "$B/rc.out" | awk '{print $6}')
    all_cited() { [ -n "$with_digit" ] && [ "$with_digit" -gt 0 ] && [ "$with_digit" = "$cited" ]; }
    check "README.md: the sentences holding a digit ($with_digit) are more than none and all cited ($cited)" "$(yn all_cited)"
    echo "command: python3 -I <scripts>/changelog_check.py <scratch>/w/CHANGELOG.md <scratch>/w/tree.txt"
    python3 -I "$CLCHECK" "$CL" "$B/tree.txt" >"$B/cc.out" 2>"$B/cc.err" </dev/null
    cc=$?
    echo "  exit: $cc; standard error $(wc -c <"$B/cc.err") bytes; standard output:"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$B/cc.out"
    cc_ok() { [ "$cc" = 0 ] && summary_is "$B/cc.out" 'uncited 0 missing 0' && [ ! -s "$B/cc.err" ]; }
    echo "  CHANGELOG.md: $(tail -n 1 "$B/cc.out")"
    check "CHANGELOG.md: every sentence holding a digit cites a records/ or specs/ path the phase commit's tree holds, the release heading exempt: exit 0, uncited 0 missing 0, standard error empty" "$(yn cc_ok)"
    cl_digit=$(tail -n 1 "$B/cc.out" | awk '{print $4}')
    cl_cited=$(tail -n 1 "$B/cc.out" | awk '{print $6}')
    cl_all_cited() { [ -n "$cl_digit" ] && [ "$cl_digit" -gt 0 ] && [ "$cl_digit" = "$cl_cited" ]; }
    check "CHANGELOG.md: the sentences holding a digit ($cl_digit) are more than none and all cited ($cl_cited)" "$(yn cl_all_cited)"
    n_exempt=$(count_matches -E '^exempt unit [0-9]+$' "$B/cc.out")
    echo "  CHANGELOG.md: exempt headings: $n_exempt"
    check "CHANGELOG.md: exactly one unit exempt, the release heading" "$(yn [ "$n_exempt" = 1 ])"
    cp "$R" "$B/planted-readme.md"
    printf '\nPlanted: the sample ran 99 times over the corpus.\n' >>"$B/planted-readme.md"
    echo "command (control): the README check over a copy of README.md with the paragraph \"Planted: the sample ran 99 times over the corpus.\" appended after a blank line"
    python3 -I "$CHECKER" "$B/planted-readme.md" "$B/tree.txt" >"$B/rcp.out" 2>"$B/rcp.err" </dev/null
    rcp=$?
    echo "  exit: $rcp; the summary: $(tail -n 1 "$B/rcp.out")"
    rcp_ok() { [ "$rcp" = 1 ] && summary_is "$B/rcp.out" 'uncited 1 missing 0'; }
    check "control: the README check refuses the planted uncited sentence" "$(yn rcp_ok)"
    cp "$CL" "$B/planted-changelog.md"
    printf -- '- Planted: the sample ran 99 times over the corpus.\n' >>"$B/planted-changelog.md"
    echo "command (control): the CHANGELOG check over a copy of CHANGELOG.md with the bullet \"- Planted: the sample ran 99 times over the corpus.\" appended"
    python3 -I "$CLCHECK" "$B/planted-changelog.md" "$B/tree.txt" >"$B/ccp.out" 2>"$B/ccp.err" </dev/null
    ccp=$?
    echo "  exit: $ccp; the summary: $(tail -n 1 "$B/ccp.out")"
    ccp_ok() { [ "$ccp" = 1 ] && summary_is "$B/ccp.out" 'uncited 1 missing 0'; }
    check "control: the CHANGELOG check refuses the planted uncited sentence (the negative log holds the fuller set)" "$(yn ccp_ok)"
    
    echo
    echo "## 2. the CHANGELOG's form (specs/0031 \"The CHANGELOG (P1)\"; rulings 1, 2, 4 and 5; change GF)"
    DASH=$(printf '\342\200\224')
    n_dash=$(count_matches -F "$DASH" "$CL")
    printf 'a %s b\n' "$DASH" >"$B/c-dash"
    n_dash_c=$(count_matches -F "$DASH" "$B/c-dash")
    echo "  command: count_matches -F <the bytes E2 80 94> <CHANGELOG> -> $n_dash; over a control line holding them -> $n_dash_c"
    check "the CHANGELOG holds no U+2014" "$(yn [ "$n_dash" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds the control's one line" "$(yn [ "$n_dash_c" = 1 ])"
    n_na=$(count_matches -P '[^\x00-\x7F]' "$CL")
    printf 'caf\303\251\n' >"$B/c-na"
    n_na_c=$(count_matches -P '[^\x00-\x7F]' "$B/c-na")
    echo "  command: count_matches -P '[^\\x00-\\x7F]' <CHANGELOG> -> $n_na; over a control line with one two-byte character -> $n_na_c"
    check "the CHANGELOG is ASCII, so UTF-8" "$(yn [ "$n_na" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds the control's one line" "$(yn [ "$n_na_c" = 1 ])"
    n_ctl=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\011\013-\037\177' <"$CL" | wc -c)
    printf 'a\tb\033c\n' >"$B/c-ctl"
    n_ctl_c=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\011\013-\037\177' <"$B/c-ctl" | wc -c)
    echo "  command: tr -cd <every control byte but line feed> <CHANGELOG> | wc -c -> $n_ctl; over a control line holding a tab and an ESC -> $n_ctl_c"
    check "the CHANGELOG holds no control byte other than line feed" "$(yn [ "$n_ctl" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds the control line's two bytes" "$(yn [ "$n_ctl_c" = 2 ])"
    max_w=$(LC_ALL=C awk 'length($0) > m { m = length($0) } END { print m + 0 }' "$CL")
    echo "  command: awk, the longest line of the CHANGELOG -> $max_w columns; p1_width_ok <CHANGELOG> 80"
    check "every line of the CHANGELOG is at most 80 columns" "$(yn p1_width_ok "$CL" 80)"
    cp "$CL" "$B/c-wide"
    printf '%081d\n' 0 >>"$B/c-wide"
    check "control: p1_width_ok refuses a copy with one line of 81 columns appended" "$(ynnot p1_width_ok "$B/c-wide" 80)"
    n_h12=$(count_matches -E '^#{1,2} ' "$CL")
    n_top=$(count_matches -xF '# Changelog' "$CL")
    n_rel=$(count_matches -xF '## 0.1.0 (unreleased)' "$CL")
    echo "  command: count_matches -E '^#{1,2} ' -> $n_h12; count_matches -xF '# Changelog' -> $n_top; count_matches -xF '## 0.1.0 (unreleased)' -> $n_rel"
    heads_ok() { [ "$n_h12" = 2 ] && [ "$n_top" = 1 ] && [ "$n_rel" = 1 ]; }
    check "one top heading \"# Changelog\", one release heading \"## 0.1.0 (unreleased)\", no other heading of level one or two" "$(yn heads_ok)"
    cp "$CL" "$B/c-heads"
    printf '\n## 0.2.0 (planted)\n' >>"$B/c-heads"
    n_h12_c=$(count_matches -E '^#{1,2} ' "$B/c-heads")
    check "control: the same count reads three headings in a copy with one more release heading planted" "$(yn [ "$n_h12_c" = 3 ])"
    WANT_SUBS='Added|Changed|Measured|Not supported by design'
    subs_of() { LC_ALL=C "$G" -E '^### ' "$1" | sed 's/^### //' | paste -s -d '|'; }
    SUBS=$(subs_of "$CL")
    echo "  command: grep -E '^### ' <CHANGELOG>, the headings joined by | -> $SUBS"
    check "the four subsections are Added, Changed, Measured and Not supported by design, in that order, and no other" "$(yn [ "$SUBS" = "$WANT_SUBS" ])"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the four in another order" "$(ynnot [ "$SUBS" = 'Changed|Added|Measured|Not supported by design' ])"
    echo "### Added"
    p1_bullets "$CL" Added "$B/added"
    rc_added=$?
    n_added=$(count_lines "$B/added")
    echo "  command: p1_bullets <CHANGELOG> Added -> exit $rc_added; bullets $n_added"
    check "the Added section holds bullets alone, eighteen of them (ruling 4 (b))" "$(yn [ "$rc_added:$n_added" = 0:18 ])"
    WANT_SPECS='0008|0009|0010|0011|0012|0013|0016|0017|0018|0019|0020|0021|0022 0028|0024|0025|0026|0027|0028'
    spec_seq() { while IFS= read -r line; do printf '%s\n' "$line" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E 'specs/[0-9]{4}-[a-z0-9-]+[.]md' | sed -E 's#^specs/([0-9]{4}).*#\1#' | paste -s -d ' '; done <"$1" | paste -s -d '|'; }
    SPECS=$(spec_seq "$B/added")
    echo "  command: per bullet, grep -o -E 'specs/[0-9]{4}-[a-z0-9-]+[.]md', the numbers joined by a space, the bullets by | -> $SPECS"
    check "each Added bullet cites its spec by its full path, in spec order, the comments-and-images bullet citing specs/0022 and specs/0028 (ruling 4)" "$(yn [ "$SPECS" = "$WANT_SPECS" ])"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the order with 0010 and 0011 swapped" "$(ynnot [ "$SPECS" = '0008|0009|0011|0010|0012|0013|0016|0017|0018|0019|0020|0021|0022 0028|0024|0025|0026|0027|0028' ])"
    echo "  (every cited path is in the phase commit's tree: the CHANGELOG check above counted missing 0)"
    awk '/^\[features\]$/ { f = 1; next } /^\[/ { f = 0 } f && /^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]* = / { print $1 }' "$B/Cargo.toml" | sort >"$B/feat-cargo"
    echo "  command: awk over <phase commit>:crates/emend/Cargo.toml, the names of the [features] table -> $(count_lines "$B/feat-cargo"): $(tr '\n' ' ' <"$B/feat-cargo")"
    n_feat_bad=0
    for f in $(cat "$B/feat-cargo"); do
      nf=$(count_matches -F "\`$f\`" "$B/added")
      echo "    \`$f\`: Added bullets naming it $nf"
      [ "$nf" -ge 1 ] || n_feat_bad=$((n_feat_bad + 1))
    done
    feat_named() { [ "$(count_lines "$B/feat-cargo")" -gt 0 ] && [ "$n_feat_bad" = 0 ]; }
    check "every feature of the manifest is named in backticks by an Added bullet" "$(yn feat_named)"
    n_feat_c=$(count_matches -F '`planted`' "$B/added")
    check "control: the same count finds no bullet naming a feature the manifest lacks" "$(yn [ "$n_feat_c" = 0 ])"
    echo "### Changed"
    p1_bullets "$CL" Changed "$B/changed"
    rc_changed=$?
    n_changed=$(count_lines "$B/changed")
    n_c30=$(count_matches -F '`specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes.md`' "$B/changed")
    echo "  command: p1_bullets <CHANGELOG> Changed -> exit $rc_changed; bullets $n_changed; of them citing specs/0030-carries-and-api-changes.md: $n_c30"
    check "the Changed section holds bullets alone, six, each citing specs/0030" "$(yn [ "$rc_changed:$n_changed:$n_c30" = 0:6:6 ])"
    WANT_CHANGED=('`SharedStringItem`, formerly `Item`' '`SharedStringItems`, formerly `Items`' '`CellKind`, formerly `Kind`' '`ContentTypeDefault`, formerly `Default`' '`HyperlinkTarget`, formerly `Target`' '`Archive::prefix_len` made crate-private')
    changed_ok() {
      local i=0 w
      for w in "${WANT_CHANGED[@]}"; do
        i=$((i + 1))
        case "$(sed -n "${i}p" "$1")" in *"$w"*) ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
      done
    }
    for i in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do
      case "$(sed -n "${i}p" "$B/changed")" in *"${WANT_CHANGED[$((i - 1))]}"*) held=yes ;; *) held=no ;; esac
      echo "    bullet $i: the expected text ${WANT_CHANGED[$((i - 1))]}; held by the CHANGELOG's bullet $i: $held"
    done
    check "the six Changed bullets name the five renames and prefix_len, in specs/0030's order, the crate root's re-export named in the third (ruling 5)" "$(yn changed_ok "$B/changed")"
    n_reexp=$(sed -n 3p "$B/changed" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -c -F "the crate root's re-export")
    check "the third Changed bullet names the crate root's re-export" "$(yn [ "$n_reexp" = 1 ])"
    { sed -n 2p "$B/changed"; sed -n 1p "$B/changed"; sed -n '3,6p' "$B/changed"; } >"$B/c-changed"
    check "control: the swapped copy differs from the bullets (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/changed" "$B/c-changed")"
    check "control: changed_ok refuses the bullets with the first two swapped" "$(ynnot changed_ok "$B/c-changed")"
    echo "### Measured (rulings 1 and 2)"
    p1_measured_equal "$CL" "$R" "$B"
    rc_meas=$?
    echo "  command: p1_measured_equal <CHANGELOG> <README> <scratch>/w -> exit $rc_meas; the joined bullets <scratch>/w/m-cl: $(count_lines "$B/m-cl") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/m-cl" | cut -d' ' -f1); the README's paragraphs <scratch>/w/m-rd: $(count_lines "$B/m-rd") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/m-rd" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
    n=0
    while IFS= read -r line; do n=$((n + 1)); echo "    paragraph $n: $(printf '%s' "$line" | wc -c) bytes"; done <"$B/m-rd"
    check "the five Measured bullets, joined, are byte for byte the README's five measured paragraphs, in order" "$(yn [ "$rc_meas" = 0 ])"
    sed 's/784 of the 917 zip entries/785 of the 917 zip entries/' "$CL" >"$B/c-meas.md"
    mkdir -p "$B/c-meas" || exit 2
    echo "command (control): the same over a copy of the CHANGELOG with one byte of a Measured bullet changed (784 to 785)"
    check "control: the copy differs from the CHANGELOG" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$CL" "$B/c-meas.md")"
    check "control: p1_measured_equal refuses the copy" "$(ynnot p1_measured_equal "$B/c-meas.md" "$R" "$B/c-meas")"
    echo "### Not supported by design (ruling 5)"
    p1_bullets "$CL" 'Not supported by design' "$B/notsup"
    rc_ns=$?
    n_ns=$(count_lines "$B/notsup")
    p1_paragraphs "$R" "$B/readme-paras"
    echo "  command: p1_bullets <CHANGELOG> 'Not supported by design' -> exit $rc_ns; bullets $n_ns; p1_paragraphs <README>: $(count_lines "$B/readme-paras") paragraphs, the second $(sed -n 2p "$B/readme-paras" | wc -c) bytes"
    check "the section holds one bullet alone" "$(yn [ "$rc_ns:$n_ns" = 0:1 ])"
    in_para2() { local n; n=$(cat "$1"); [ -n "$n" ] || return 1; case "$(sed -n 2p "$B/readme-paras")" in *"$n"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }
    check "the bullet, joined, is a part of the README's second paragraph, joined (the openpyxl limits in the README's words)" "$(yn in_para2 "$B/notsup")"
    sed 's/does not render/does not render charts/' "$B/notsup" >"$B/c-notsup"
    check "control: the planted copy differs from the bullet (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/notsup" "$B/c-notsup")"
    check "control: the same test refuses the bullet with two words planted" "$(ynnot in_para2 "$B/c-notsup")"
    
    echo
    echo "## 3. the crate-level doc comment (specs/0031 \"The crate-level doc comment (decision 3)\"; change GG)"
    p1_docblock "$B/lib.rs" "$B/db"
    p1_paragraphs "$B/db" "$B/db-paras"
    p1_docblock "$B/lib-base.rs" "$B/db-base"
    p1_paragraphs "$B/db-base" "$B/db-base-paras"
    echo "  command: p1_docblock and p1_paragraphs over lib.rs at the phase commit -> $(count_lines "$B/db") lines, $(count_lines "$B/db-paras") paragraphs; at the base -> $(count_lines "$B/db-base") lines, $(count_lines "$B/db-base-paras") paragraphs"
    sed -n 2,3p "$B/db-paras" >"$B/db-23"
    sed -n 1,2p "$B/readme-paras" >"$B/rd-12"
    check "the doc comment's second and third paragraphs, joined, are the README's first two (what emend is; the openpyxl comparison)" "$(yn cmp -s "$B/db-23" "$B/rd-12")"
    sed 's/openpyxl/openpyxI/' "$B/rd-12" >"$B/c-rd-12"
    check "control: the changed copy differs from the README's two (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/rd-12" "$B/c-rd-12")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the README's two with one byte changed" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/db-23" "$B/c-rd-12")"
    DEP=$(sed -n 3p "$B/readme-paras" | sed -E 's/([.]) .*/\1/')
    echo "  the README's dependency sentence (its third paragraph to the first full stop and space): $DEP"
    dep_in_doc() { [ -n "$1" ] || return 1; case "$(sed -n 4p "$B/db-paras")" in "$1"*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }
    check "the doc comment's fourth paragraph opens with the README's dependency sentence" "$(yn dep_in_doc "$DEP")"
    DEP_C="${DEP%nothing else.}nothing else at all."
    check "control: the planted sentence differs from the README's" "$(ynnot [ "$DEP_C" = "$DEP" ])"
    check "control: the same test refuses the sentence with a word planted" "$(ynnot dep_in_doc "$DEP_C")"
    LC_ALL=C "$G" -o -E '^- `[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*`: ' "$B/db" | sed -E 's/^- `([^`]*)`: $/\1/' | sort >"$B/feat-doc"
    echo "  command: grep -o -E '^- \`<name>\`: ' over the doc comment -> $(count_lines "$B/feat-doc"): $(tr '\n' ' ' <"$B/feat-doc")"
    check "one line per feature, the set the manifest's [features] names, none twice" "$(yn cmp -s "$B/feat-cargo" "$B/feat-doc")"
    { cat "$B/feat-doc"; echo planted; } | sort >"$B/c-feat-doc"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the doc comment's list with one feature more" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/feat-cargo" "$B/c-feat-doc")"
    CODE2_DIFF=$B/code2.diff
    git diff "$CODE2^" "$CODE2" >"$CODE2_DIFF"
    echo "command: git diff <the doc comment's commit>^ <the doc comment's commit> > <scratch>/w/code2.diff: $(count_lines "$CODE2_DIFF") lines, sha256 $(sha256sum <"$CODE2_DIFF" | cut -d' ' -f1); files: $(git diff --name-only "$CODE2^" "$CODE2" | tr '\n' ' ')"
    n_add_doc=$(count_matches -E '^[+]//!' "$CODE2_DIFF")
    n_add_dig=$(count_matches -E '^[+]//!.*[0-9]' "$CODE2_DIFF")
    echo "  command: count_matches -E '^[+]//!' -> $n_add_doc added doc lines; count_matches -E '^[+]//!.*[0-9]' -> $n_add_dig of them holding a digit"
    no_digit_added() { [ "$n_add_doc" -gt 0 ] && [ "$n_add_dig" = 0 ]; }
    check "the commit adds doc lines, and none of them holds a digit (decision 3: no figure)" "$(yn no_digit_added)"
    cp "$CODE2_DIFF" "$B/c-code2-digit.diff"
    printf '+//! planted 12 figures\n' >>"$B/c-code2-digit.diff"
    n_add_dig_c=$(count_matches -E '^[+]//!.*[0-9]' "$B/c-code2-digit.diff")
    check "control: the same count finds the one added doc line planted with a digit" "$(yn [ "$n_add_dig_c" = 1 ])"
    check "every line the commit adds or removes is a doc line (p1_doc_lines_only)" "$(yn p1_doc_lines_only "$CODE2_DIFF")"
    awk '{ print } /^@@ / && !done { print "+fn planted() {}"; done = 1 }' "$CODE2_DIFF" >"$B/c-code2-code.diff"
    check "control: the planted copy differs from the diff (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$CODE2_DIFF" "$B/c-code2-code.diff")"
    check "control: p1_doc_lines_only refuses a copy with one code line planted after the first hunk header" "$(ynnot p1_doc_lines_only "$B/c-code2-code.diff")"
    : >"$B/c-empty.diff"
    check "control: p1_doc_lines_only refuses an empty diff" "$(ynnot p1_doc_lines_only "$B/c-empty.diff")"
    n_kept_bad=0
    for pre in 'The acceptance instrument is the fidelity harness' 'Every zip offset, entry size, byte count and file position'; do
      LC_ALL=C awk -v p="$pre" 'index($0, p) == 1' "$B/db-paras" >"$B/kept-new"
      LC_ALL=C awk -v p="$pre" 'index($0, p) == 1' "$B/db-base-paras" >"$B/kept-base"
      echo "  the paragraph opening \"$pre\": at the phase commit $(count_lines "$B/kept-new"), sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/kept-new" | cut -d' ' -f1); at the base $(count_lines "$B/kept-base"), sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/kept-base" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      if [ "$(count_lines "$B/kept-new")" = 1 ] && [ "$(count_lines "$B/kept-base")" = 1 ] && cmp -s "$B/kept-new" "$B/kept-base"; then :; else n_kept_bad=$((n_kept_bad + 1)); fi
    done
    check "the paragraphs on the harness and on u64 stay, each once and equal to the base's" "$(yn [ "$n_kept_bad" = 0 ])"
    sed 's/u64/u32/' "$B/kept-new" >"$B/c-kept"
    check "control: the changed copy differs from the paragraph (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/kept-new" "$B/c-kept")"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the u64 paragraph with one word changed" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/c-kept" "$B/kept-base")"
    
    echo
    echo "## 4. the licence copies and the readme line (specs/0031 \"The licence texts and the README in the package (decision 4)\"; ruling 3; change GH)"
    n_cmp_bad=0
    for l in MIT APACHE; do
      cmp "$B/LICENSE-$l" "$B/crate-LICENSE-$l" >"$B/cmp-$l.out" 2>&1
      rcl=$?
      echo "  command: cmp <phase commit>:LICENSE-$l <phase commit>:crates/emend/LICENSE-$l -> exit $rcl, output $(wc -c <"$B/cmp-$l.out") bytes; sha256 $(sha256sum <"$B/LICENSE-$l" | cut -d' ' -f1) and $(sha256sum <"$B/crate-LICENSE-$l" | cut -d' ' -f1)"
      [ "$rcl" = 0 ] || n_cmp_bad=$((n_cmp_bad + 1))
    done
    check "each licence text in crates/emend/ is byte for byte the repository root's" "$(yn [ "$n_cmp_bad" = 0 ])"
    check "control: cmp refuses the MIT text against the Apache one" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/LICENSE-MIT" "$B/crate-LICENSE-APACHE")"
    n_readme=$(count_matches -xF 'readme = "../../README.md"' "$B/Cargo.toml")
    n_pub=$(count_matches -xF 'publish = false' "$B/Cargo.toml")
    echo "  command: count_matches -xF 'readme = \"../../README.md\"' <phase commit>:crates/emend/Cargo.toml -> $n_readme; count_matches -xF 'publish = false' -> $n_pub"
    check "the manifest holds the readme line once and publish = false once" "$(yn [ "$n_readme:$n_pub" = 1:1 ])"
    readme_after_publish() { LC_ALL=C awk 'prev == "publish = false" && $0 == "readme = \"../../README.md\"" { ok = 1 } { prev = $0 } END { exit ok ? 0 : 1 }' "$1"; }
    check "the readme line follows publish = false directly (ruling 3)" "$(yn readme_after_publish "$B/Cargo.toml")"
    { LC_ALL=C "$G" -v -x -F 'readme = "../../README.md"' "$B/Cargo.toml"; echo 'readme = "../../README.md"'; } >"$B/c-Cargo.toml"
    check "control: the moved copy differs from the manifest (cmp)" "$(ynnot cmp -s "$B/Cargo.toml" "$B/c-Cargo.toml")"
    check "control: readme_after_publish refuses a copy with the line moved to the end" "$(ynnot readme_after_publish "$B/c-Cargo.toml")"
    
    echo
    echo "## the log itself"
    ALL=()
    for f in "$B"/*.out "$B"/*.err; do ALL+=("$f"); done
    nbytes=$(cat "${ALL[@]}" | LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' | wc -c)
    printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m y caf\303\251\n' >"$B/ctl-bytes"
    nctl=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd '\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177\200-\377' <"$B/ctl-bytes" | wc -c)
    echo "  captured outputs: ${#ALL[@]}; control and non-ASCII bytes in them: $nbytes; in a control file with two ESC and one two-byte character: $nctl"
    check "no control or non-ASCII byte in any captured output" "$(yn [ "$nbytes" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count finds four bytes in the control file" "$(yn [ "$nctl" = 4 ])"
    echo "command: git status --porcelain"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    st_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    st_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS" git status --porcelain)
    echo "status lines: $st_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's two files: $st_ours"
    status_accounted() { [ "$st_all" != error ] && [ "$st_ours" != error ] && [ "$st_all" = "$st_ours" ]; }
    check "every change in the working tree is this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec and ROADMAP.md" "$(yn status_accounted)"
    echo
    echo "## this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    nerr=$(wc -c <"$ERR")
    cp "$ERR" "$B/c-err"; printf 'x' >>"$B/c-err"
    nerr_ctl=$(wc -c <"$B/c-err")
    echo "  command: wc -c <scripts>/out/run.stderr: $nerr bytes; a copy with one byte appended: $nerr_ctl"
    check "this script wrote nothing to its standard error" "$(yn [ "$nerr" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count reads one byte more in the copy" "$(yn [ "$nerr_ctl" = "$((nerr + 1))" ])"
    echo "run ended at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "run log checks all as expected: $([ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    exec 1>&3
    wait "$SCRUB_PID" 2>/dev/null
    exit "$fail"

## the shared counting library this log sources
    # emend shared counting forms (specs/0007-gate-self-test.md, "scripts/counting.sh").
    #
    # Sourced by scripts/gate-self-test.sh and by the log scripts of later phases,
    # which use these instead of carrying their own copy.
    #
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. In T3 P4 four counting sites compared a bare `grep -c`
    # with 0 without reading the exit status, so a read error printed 0 and the
    # check passed. Two independent checks found them, and the class was declared
    # closed three further times while instances still stood: a helper whose first
    # repair read grep's status while `cat` was the failing command, and both
    # halves of one function in T2 P7. Planning's remedy is that every counting
    # form, including a throwaway one, is a named function that reads the exit
    # status and has a positive control, from the start.
    #
    # Every function prints either a number or the word "error". None of them ever
    # prints a matched line, a value, or a path. A caller that compares a result
    # with 0 therefore cannot mistake a failed read for an absence.
    #
    # counting_controls runs every form against a match, a miss and an unreadable
    # file, prints one line per control, and sets counting_controls_ok. Callers run
    # it before using any form.
    
    G=/usr/bin/grep
    
    # count_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: total matching lines across the
    # files, or "error" if any file could not be read. The files go to grep
    # directly: with `cat file | grep -c`, an unreadable file makes CAT fail while
    # grep counts 0 over the empty stream and exits 1, so the read error reads as
    # "no hits".
    count_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_from_file <-F|-E> <pattern file> <file>...: the same, with the patterns
    # read from a file (grep -f), so a pattern is never printed.
    count_from_file() {
      local mode=$1 patterns=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -f "$patterns" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_bytes <tr class> <file>...: total bytes of that class across the files,
    # or "error" if any file could not be read. Each file is read on its own rather
    # than through `cat "$@" | tr`, for the same reason as above.
    count_bytes() {
      local class=$1 total=0 f n
      shift
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd "$class" <"$f" | wc -c) || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_lines <file>...: total lines, or "error" if any file could not be read.
    count_lines() {
      local total=0 f n
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(wc -l <"$f") || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_matches_i <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: count_matches with -i, the form
    # the hygiene step uses for OFFICE_RE and ZONE_RE. It lived in a log script as a
    # hand-rolled copy with no control of its own (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap
    # 14); a counting form belongs here, with a control, even a throwaway one.
    count_matches_i() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c -i "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # run_lines <command...>: run the command, and print the number of lines it
    # wrote on stdout, or "error" if the command itself failed. The log scripts
    # asserted a clean working tree with
    #   git status --porcelain >file 2>/dev/null || : >file
    # which turns a FAILED git status into an empty file, so the check read a
    # failed command as a clean tree (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap 1; planning
    # reproduced it). Reading the command's exit status is the whole point: a
    # command that could not run is never the same as a command that found nothing.
    run_lines() {
      local out rc
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l | tr -d ' '
    }
    
    # run_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <command...>: the same, counting only the lines
    # that match, so a caller can compare "all lines" with "lines of the expected
    # shape" without either count hiding a failed command.
    run_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" || true
    }
    
    CTRL_CLASS='\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177'
    NONASCII_CLASS='\200-\377'
    
    # counting_controls <label prefix>: run every counting form above against a
    # fixture that must match and one that must not, and against an unreadable
    # file, printing one line per result. Sets counting_controls_ok to yes or no.
    # This is the positive control planning requires for every form, and it also
    # proves each form reports "error" rather than 0 when it cannot read.
    counting_controls() {
      local d ok=yes r
      d=$(mktemp -d) || { counting_controls_ok=no; return; }
      printf 'alpha\nbeta needle gamma\nneedle\n' >"$d/hit"
      printf 'nothing of interest\n' >"$d/miss"
      printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m\017 y\ncaf\303\251\n' >"$d/bytes"
      printf 'needle\n' >"$d/patterns"
      printf 'unreadable\n' >"$d/locked"
      chmod 000 "$d/locked"
    
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit");        echo "  control count_matches on a file with two matching lines: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/miss");       echo "  control count_matches on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_matches summed over two files: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -E 'ne+dle' "$d/hit");      echo "  control count_matches with a regular expression: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/locked");     echo "  control count_matches on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_matches on readable plus unreadable: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/hit"); echo "  control count_from_file on the same file: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_from_file on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_from_file on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/bytes");    echo "  control count_bytes, control bytes in a file with ESC, ESC and SI: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$NONASCII_CLASS" "$d/bytes"); echo "  control count_bytes, non-ASCII bytes in a file with one two-byte character: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/miss");     echo "  control count_bytes on a clean file: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_bytes on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/hit");                    echo "  control count_lines on a three-line file: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/locked");                 echo "  control count_lines on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      printf 'NEEDLE\nneedle\n' >"$d/mixedcase"
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/mixedcase"); echo "  control count_matches_i on mixed case: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/mixedcase");  echo "  control count_matches on the same file, case sensitive: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_matches_i on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines printf 'a\nb\nc\n');            echo "  control run_lines on a command printing three lines: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines true);                          echo "  control run_lines on a command printing nothing: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines false);                         echo "  control run_lines on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines "$d/no-such-command");          echo "  control run_lines on a command that cannot run: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle printf 'needle\nother\n'); echo "  control run_matches on a command printing one match: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle false);             echo "  control run_matches on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
    
      chmod 644 "$d/locked"
      rm -rf "${d:?}"
      counting_controls_ok=$ok
      echo "  counting controls: $([ "$ok" = yes ] && echo "all as expected" || echo FAILED)"
    }

